<main id="main">
<div class="wrap">

  <header class="hero hero--page">
    <div class="eyebrow-rule" style="margin-bottom:clamp(26px,4vh,44px)"><span class="eyebrow">About</span></div>
    <h1 class="display h1--page">Making a life of becoming</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="grid grid--about" style="padding:0 0 clamp(60px,9vh,110px)">
    <div data-reveal>
      <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/about/blossom.jpg" alt="Faye standing under cherry blossom" fetchpriority="high"></div>
    </div>
    <div data-reveal style="max-width:60ch">
      <p class="lede">I’m Faye Wai — an artist, poet, and curious human from Hong Kong, now based in Port Moody, British Columbia.</p>
      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:clamp(22px,3vh,34px)">
        <p>For most of my life, I thought I was supposed to figure out what I wanted to be, choose a direction, and become very good at moving toward it.</p>
        <p>The problem was: I was much better at achieving than knowing.</p>
        <p>These days, I’m less interested in building a perfectly linear life. I’m learning to follow curiosity, become a beginner again, and leave enough space for the next version of myself to emerge.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="pull" style="margin-top:clamp(30px,4.5vh,44px)">There is always more to become.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--rule grid grid--2" style="padding:clamp(50px,8vh,100px) 0" aria-labelledby="path-h">
    <div data-reveal>
      <h2 class="h2" id="path-h" style="max-width:15ch">I used to think the path was supposed to be obvious</h2>
      <div class="frame frame--round r43" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,44px);max-width:420px"><img src="assets/about/newsroom.jpg" alt="Faye at work in a broadcast control room, autocue on screen" loading="lazy"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal style="max-width:52ch">
      <p>I never really knew what I wanted to do in school — which mostly made me try harder to figure it out.</p>
      <p>At university, I did seven internships. I collected experience, credentials, bylines, opportunities — anything that might help the path reveal itself. I became very comfortable with measurable progress: the next grade, the next role, the next thing I could point to and say, <em>See? I’m getting somewhere.</em></p>
      <p>Somewhere along the way, I also learned a quieter story about creativity. Art was the only B on my high-school scorecard. It seems almost absurd now, but for years I carried that as evidence that perhaps I simply wasn’t creative.</p>
      <p>So I leaned into the things I knew how to succeed at.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--rule" style="padding:clamp(50px,8vh,100px) 0" aria-labelledby="lives-h">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2 class="h2" id="lives-h" style="max-width:15ch">Curiosity has taken me through a few lives</h2>
        <div class="kv kv--narrow" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,40px);max-width:44ch">
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Journalism</span><span>taught me to ask.</span></div>
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Wine</span><span>taught me to notice.</span></div>
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Editorial</span><span>taught me to find the thread.</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="prose" style="margin-top:22px;max-width:42ch">I don’t think of those chapters as detours anymore. I carry all of them with me.</p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <div class="prose" style="max-width:52ch">
          <p>I began in journalism, drawn to stories and the strange privilege of being allowed to ask people questions for a living. From there came communications, editorial work, technology, strategy — years spent understanding people, ideas, and how one becomes meaningful to the other.</p>
          <p>Then there was wine. What started as curiosity became study, certifications, vineyards, harvests, and an entirely new language for paying attention. Wine taught me to notice weather, geography, time, agriculture, scent — to understand how a place leaves traces of itself behind.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tiles tiles--2" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,40px);max-width:460px">
          <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/photos/vineyard.jpg" alt="Faye harvesting grapes by hand in a vineyard" loading="lazy"></div>
          <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/photos/cellar.jpg" alt="Faye in a winery cellar beside steel tanks" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 40%"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--rule" style="padding:clamp(50px,8vh,100px) 0 clamp(60px,9vh,110px)" aria-labelledby="motion-h">
    <div class="grid grid--2" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <h2 class="h2" id="motion-h" style="max-width:13ch">A life in motion</h2>
        <div class="frame frame--round r34" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,44px);max-width:320px"><img src="assets/photos/mount-rainier.jpg" alt="Faye on a trail below Mount Rainier" loading="lazy"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:52ch">
        <p>I’ve always been restless in the best way — curious about what another place, another field, another version of life might teach me.</p>
        <p>I grew up in Hong Kong, spent a year studying in Paris, built a career through journalism and storytelling, followed an obsession with wine all the way to the Okanagan, and later traded city life for slower stretches on Vancouver Island.</p>
        <p>Each move gave me another way of seeing. I used to think I was searching for the place or path that would finally make everything click. Now I think I was simply learning how many different ways there are to live.</p>
        <span class="eyebrow eyebrow--faint" style="display:block;margin-top:clamp(30px,4.5vh,44px)">Retracing recent relocations</span>
        <div class="kv kv--wide" style="margin-top:16px">
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Okanagan Valley</span><span>A season full of wildflowers and wildfires, of harvesting grapes by hand.</span></div>
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Vancouver Island</span><span>Stretches of quiet along the coastline, living two minutes from the beach.</span></div>
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">Port Moody</span><span>Settling in a cozy community beside mountains, forest and water.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>

<section class="panel panel--rule" aria-labelledby="pause-h">
  <div class="panel__in grid grid--origin">
    <div data-reveal>
      <span class="eyebrow">Origin</span>
      <h2 class="h2" id="pause-h" style="font-size:clamp(30px,3.6vw,54px);line-height:1.05;margin-top:22px;max-width:16ch">Finding power in pause</h2>
      <div class="frame frame--round r34" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,44px);max-width:300px"><img src="assets/about/jai.jpg" alt="Jai, Faye’s small white dog, sitting in grass scattered with blossom" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 60%"></div>
      <div class="tiles tiles--2" style="margin-top:clamp(16px,2.4vh,24px);max-width:300px">
        <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/photos/ocean-sunset.jpg" alt="Faye on a shoreline stairway at sunset" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/photos/dallas-road.jpg" alt="Faye among wildflowers on Dallas Road" loading="lazy"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div data-reveal style="max-width:58ch">
      <p class="lede" style="font-size:clamp(19px,2vw,26px);line-height:1.45">Grief was one of the things that finally made me stop.</p>
      <div class="prose prose--dark" style="margin-top:22px">
        <p>After losing Jai, my dog and constant companion through my teens and twenties, something in me shifted. I couldn’t keep moving through life at quite the same speed. I started giving myself permission to wander — geographically, creatively, and inwardly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose prose--dark" style="margin-top:26px">
        <p>What I thought might be a pause started teaching me another way to live. Creativity returned not as another thing to optimise, but as a form of attention. A way to process. A way to play. A way to hear myself more clearly when I stopped filling every available space with forward motion.</p>
        <p>These days, I’m learning to live beyond job titles and metrics.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="wrap">

  <section style="padding:clamp(60px,9vh,110px) 0 clamp(50px,8vh,100px)" aria-labelledby="beginner-h">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2 class="h2" id="beginner-h" style="max-width:14ch">Becoming a beginner again</h2>
        <div class="prose" style="margin-top:clamp(22px,3vh,34px);max-width:46ch">
          <p>Eventually, I found my way back to the thing I had quietly decided years ago I wasn’t very good at.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="pull" style="font-size:clamp(26px,3vw,40px);margin-top:18px">Painting.</p>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <div class="prose" style="max-width:52ch">
          <p>At first, being a beginner was uncomfortable. There was no promotion to earn, no metric telling me whether I was progressing fast enough, no clean answer to whether something was “good.” And slowly, that became the point.</p>
          <p>My creative work is intuitive, sometimes messy, and always honest. Painting asks me to slow down enough to respond brush by brush. Poetry gives all the tangled thoughts somewhere to go.</p>
          <p>I’m learning to make before I know where something is going. To notice when a piece begins to shift. To trust what interests me before I can justify it.</p>
          <p>There is a freedom in doing something not because you’re already good at it, but because you feel more alive while doing it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="tiles" data-reveal style="margin-top:clamp(34px,5vh,56px);max-width:820px">
      <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/about/easel-1.jpg" alt="Faye painting at an easel in a studio" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 22%"></div>
      <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/about/easel-2.jpg" alt="Faye seated at an easel, mid-brushstroke" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 18%"></div>
      <div class="frame frame--round r34"><img src="assets/about/studio.jpg" alt="Faye in her studio, surrounded by finished paintings" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 32%"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- The rows come from _data/curious.js. -#}
  <section class="section--rule" style="padding:clamp(50px,8vh,100px) 0 clamp(60px,9vh,110px)" aria-labelledby="curious-h">
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2 class="h2" id="curious-h" style="max-width:13ch">Right now, I’m curious about…</h2>
        <div class="frame frame--round r34" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,44px);max-width:330px"><img src="assets/about/zinnias.jpg" alt="Faye standing in front of a bed of zinnias and cosmos" loading="lazy" style="object-position:center 40%"></div>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <div class="kv kv--narrow">
          {%- for c in curious %}
          <div class="kv__row"><span class="label">{{ c.label }}</span><span>{{ c.text }}</span></div>
          {%- endfor %}
        </div>
        <div class="prose" style="margin-top:clamp(28px,4vh,40px);max-width:52ch">
          <p>Outside the studio, I’m usually learning something I didn’t know I needed to learn, wandering through a gallery, singing, planning travel around food, or researching a question far beyond the amount the situation requires.</p>
          <p><strong>I’m still figuring out what a creative life can look like. I hope I never completely finish.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>

